# OneSignal Shared KMP Module Guidelines

This repo is an internal KMP repo (single source of truth) consumed by the Android and iOS SDKs
as a git submodule pinned to a tag. It is NOT published publicly (well — the Android host does
republish it to Maven Central as `com.onesignal:kmp` so `:core`'s POM resolves, but it is not a
standalone public product). Both SDKs release in lockstep for parity: tag this repo, then bump
the submodule pointer in both SDK repos.

## Module structure
- ONE umbrella module, `:kmp` (directory `kmp/`), published as `com.onesignal:kmp`. New shared
  features are added as sibling PACKAGES inside this module (e.g. `com.onesignal.logger`,
  `com.onesignal.common`, `com.onesignal.notifications`), NOT as new Gradle subprojects. Keeping
  it a single module means iOS links exactly one framework and the host wires up one artifact.
- All logic lives in `commonMain`/`commonTest`. Targets: `androidTarget`, `iosX64`, `iosArm64`, `iosSimulatorArm64`.
- The `kmp/build.gradle` must resolve under BOTH this repo's root and a host SDK root: read shared versions from the root (`rootProject.buildVersions`, `$coroutinesVersion`) and `apply from: '../spotless.gradle'`. Do not hardcode versions.
- Prefer package-level separation over new modules. Only introduce a second Gradle module if a
  feature genuinely needs different dependencies/targets; the default is a new package in `:kmp`.

## Public API = the interop contract (ports & adapters)
- Expose a SMALL public surface: interfaces + a factory + plain data classes. Mark everything else `internal` (internal never appears in the generated Obj-C header).
- Inject platform specifics via interfaces (e.g. `ILoggerPlatformProvider`, `ILogHttpSender`, `ILogFileStore`, `ILogger`); host SDKs implement the "actuals". Never put Android/iOS/JVM-only types in common.
- Compose via a factory (e.g. `LoggerFactory`), not hidden singletons.
- Use `expect/actual` only for small low-level platform primitives (see `Platform.android.kt` / `Platform.ios.kt`: `epochNanosNow`, `randomUuidString`).

## iOS / Obj-C boundary rules (Swift sees an Obj-C header)
Android consumes Kotlin directly and is never the constraint. Design the public API for the iOS boundary:
- Annotate every throwing boundary API with `@Throws`, or an uncaught Kotlin exception crashes the app (only `@Throws` becomes a Swift `throws`).
- Prefer `suspend` (bridges to Swift `async`) over exposing `Flow`/`StateFlow` at the boundary.
- Avoid at the public edge: default arguments, sealed hierarchies, generics — they bridge poorly. Provide explicit overloads/types instead.
- Keep data models as plain data classes / value-like types.
- Consider SKIE (Touchlab) later if idiomatic Swift (enums, `AsyncSequence`, `async`) is needed.
- iOS consumption needs a framework/XCFramework packaging block (`binaries.framework { baseName = ... }` or CocoaPods/SPM); add it in the iOS phase.

## Testing & build
- Put logic tests in `commonTest` so they run on every target; keep the module dependency-light (no serialization/networking libs in common).
- Verify before pushing: `./gradlew :kmp:testDebugUnitTest :kmp:iosSimulatorArm64Test spotlessCheck`.
- CI runs on a macOS runner (required for the iOS simulator tests).
